    "claims": [
      {
        "text": "Schwab earns on client assets primarily through net interest revenue from uninvested client cash swept to its bank, plus asset management and administration fees, trading revenue, and bank deposit account fees (SCHW-10K-2025#27).",
        "chunk_ids": [
          "SCHW-10K-2025#27"
        ],
        "part_id": "a1"
      },
      {
        "text": "Advisor-led firms instead earn most revenue as asset-based fees on the account balance, such as Edward Jones advisory program fees tied to average daily market value (EDJ-10K-2025#126) and Morgan Stanley's fee-based assets priced in basis points (MS-10K-2025#147).",
        "chunk_ids": [
          "EDJ-10K-2025#126",
          "MS-10K-2025#147"
        ],
        "part_id": "a2"
      },
      {
        "text": "Schwab paid only 0.01% to 0.15% on sweep cash while generating roughly $485.7 billion of net interest revenue on those balances in 2Q26 (SCHW-TRADE-2026-10-07-RB-01#4, SCHW-TRADE-2026-09-30-IN-01#1).",
        "chunk_ids": [
          "SCHW-TRADE-2026-10-07-RB-01#4",
          "SCHW-TRADE-2026-09-30-IN-01#1"
        ],
        "part_id": "a3"
      },
      {
        "text": "By contrast, advisor-led firms rely on deposit rates and yields well above Schwab's sweep rates, such as GWIM's 2.07% rate paid on deposits in 3Q26 (BAC-DECK-2026Q3#30).",
        "chunk_ids": [
          "BAC-DECK-2026Q3#30"
        ],
        "part_id": "a4"
      },
      {
        "text": "The revenue-yield comparison cannot be made across all firms on this evidence: Schwab reports roughly 21 bps revenue on client assets (SCHW-DECK-2026-investor-day#20), Raymond James notes its net basis points on assets are lower than a pure RIA (RJF-TR-2024Q3#91), but Morgan Stanley, Edward Jones, and Merrill figures were not found.",
        "chunk_ids": [
          "SCHW-DECK-2026-investor-day#20",
          "RJF-TR-2024Q3#91"
        ],
        "part_id": "a15"
      }
    ],
